\section{Threshold Sensitivity Analysis}
\label{sec:tau_sweep}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Threshold sensitivity on Goal suite (seed 42, $\varepsilon{=}0.2$). VLA-only baseline: 83.5\% (seed-matched). Optimal performance at $\tau{=}0.15$; the selected operating point $\tau{=}0.25$ trades peak single-seed improvement for a more conservative trigger rate.}
\label{tab:tau_sweep}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
$\tau$ & Trigger rate (\%) & SR (\%) & $\Delta$ vs VLA-only \\
\midrule
0.10 & 73.9 & 81.0 & $-2.5$ \\
0.15 & 33.9 & 85.0 & $+1.5$ \\
0.25 & 7--9 & 84.0 & $+0.5$ \\
0.40 & 1.2 & 83.5 & $0.0$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\looseness=-1 Table~\ref{tab:tau_sweep} reports success rates on Goal (seed 42) as a function of the uncertainty threshold $\tau$, with $\varepsilon{=}0.2$ (the adaptive correction magnitude). Performance follows an inverted-U pattern: at $\tau{=}0.10$ the gate triggers on 73.9\% of steps, overcorrecting confident ones and degrading performance by $-$2.5\,pp---reproducing the instability of unconditional correction. At $\tau{=}0.40$ the gate triggers on only 1.2\% of steps, effectively disabling the CRM and recovering VLA-only performance ($\Delta{=}0.0$).

\looseness=-1 The optimal operating region is $\tau \in [0.15, 0.25]$, where 8--34\% of steps receive correction and success rate improves by $+$0.5 to $+$1.5\,pp over the seed-matched baseline. The main experiments use $\tau{=}0.25$, which sacrifices peak single-seed improvement for a more conservative trigger rate. The inverted-U shape confirms that gating selectivity---not correction strength---drives performance: too much correction is as harmful as too little.

\looseness=-1 \textbf{Note on $\varepsilon$ comparisons.}\enspace The $\tau$-sweep uses $\varepsilon{=}0.2$ (the adaptive pipeline's norm bound). CRM always-on in Table~\ref{tab:main} uses a tighter $\varepsilon{=}0.05$ to limit overcorrection when applied unconditionally. The two settings are not directly comparable: CRM always-on ($\varepsilon{=}0.05$, 100\% trigger) achieves 85.5\% on Goal seed 42, exceeding $\tau{=}0.10$ ($\varepsilon{=}0.2$, 73.9\% trigger) at 81.0\%. This reflects the interaction between correction magnitude and trigger frequency---unconditional correction requires a gentler $\varepsilon$ to remain stable, while gated correction tolerates a larger $\varepsilon$ because it fires selectively.

